{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-84671",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of balloons that will not burst. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=20$ balloons\n\n- the probability of not bursting each balloon is $p=90%=0.9$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(20,0.9).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the mean is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the mean of the number of balloons that do not burst is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =(20)(0.9) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
